docs/multimodal.md:1:# Multimodal (VLM) Support
docs/multimodal.md:3:Prime-RL supports training vision-language models (VLMs) like Qwen3-VL.
docs/multimodal.md:5:## VLM Configuration
docs/multimodal.md:14:| Qwen3.5 | `qwen3_5` | `model.visual` | `model.language_model` |
docs/multimodal.md:15:| Qwen3.5-MoE | `qwen3_5_moe` | `model.visual` | `model.language_model` |
docs/multimodal.md:17:Enable VLM mode by adding a `[model.vlm]` section. Both fields are required — they tell prime-rl where the vision encoder and language model live on the model object:
docs/multimodal.md:28:For the registered models in the table above, use the attrs shown there. For custom VLMs, check your model's structure with `model.named_children()`.
docs/multimodal.md:34:To add permanent support for a new model family, add an entry to `VLM_REGISTRY` in `src/prime_rl/utils/vlm.py`.
docs/multimodal.md:40:- **No multimodal-safe truncation**: Token sequences are truncated to `seq_len`, but `pixel_values` and `image_grid_thw` are passed through unchanged. If a multimodal sample exceeds `seq_len`, image tokens can be dropped while image tensors still describe the full set of images. Ensure `seq_len` covers your longest VLM samples.
docs/multimodal.md:44:- **Higher KL mismatch with multi-image inputs**: VLM training exhibits higher KL mismatch compared to text-only, especially with multiple images.
docs/multimodal.md:46:- **Images are not logged**: The images the VLM sees during training are not logged to monitors.
docs/multimodal.md:48:## How Multi-Turn VLM RL Training Works
docs/multimodal.md:50:VLM rollouts go through the renderer-backed TITO client (`orchestrator.use_renderer = true`, the default and required for VLMs). The renderer owns the HuggingFace processor per-slot and emits multimodal tensors alongside tokens.
docs/multimodal.md:54:3. **Forward**: The trainer `**`-unpacks `mm_kwargs` into the model's `forward` signature, so any VLM whose HF processor and forward agree on kwarg names works without touching the transport.
docs/multimodal.md:58:## vLLM Configuration
docs/multimodal.md:60:`VLLM_WORKER_MULTIPROC_METHOD=spawn` is required for VLM inference. This is set automatically when using `uv run rl @ ...`, but if you start the vLLM server yourself, make sure this environment variable is set.
docs/slurm.md:76:**Inference** runs independent vLLM replicas per node:
docs/slurm.md:211:Run a vLLM server on a single allocated node:
docs/slurm.md:233:Each node runs an independent vLLM replica. TP and DP must fit within a single node — there is no cross-node parallelism.
